---- plaintext ----
{"user":"johndoe","email":"johndoe@example.com","ssn":"123-45-6789","balance":4200}

---- whole-document envelope ----
{"v":"vyto-jsonc-v1","kid":"2026-07","id":"user:42","alg":"ChaCha20-Poly1305","nonce":"AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAB","ct":"3tWSoEV7oDuv+IB2SEp99LGUqJk92LyJ0ykcFEle2STzAFrmMrU80fSl0gs0PN1k7XtIGsevQZqShN0zyirLNyGhDsEmrLSHOFYH7ZBCDb9iszSHAOwgTjwdI27nYIOek2V1"}

the id, key id and nonce are readable; everything about the user is not

decrypts       true (want true)
round-trips    true (want true)
email recovered johndoe@example.com

---- what an attacker with the database can try ----
record id edited      false (want false)
key id edited         false (want false)
one flipped bit       false (want false)
truncated ciphertext  false (want false)
wrong master key      false (want false)

every one fails at the tag, before a single byte of plaintext exists

---- field-level: keep the record queryable ----
{"id":"user:42","created":"2026-07-29","email":"AAAAAAAAAAAAAAACHrffStor/ZGOCZLpt83Hg2X/ixZc8HQ9dSIcT2TgZUazq5I=","ssn":"AAAAAAAAAAAAAAADjT9rmxhVeJwxct18kWcgBmqn4VvTcgnBUQjD"}

id and created are still usable by the database; the rest is opaque

email decrypts to  johndoe@example.com
ssn decrypts to    123-45-6789

---- field binding ----
ssn read as email     "" (want empty)
moved to another row  "" (want empty)

without the field name and record id in the AAD both of these would
decrypt perfectly, and the attacker would not even need the key

---- key separation ----
email subkey    3ea80c5eaf579391099b5373d137a509…
ssn subkey      4d849377be5f8f9e98a710193b2cd1ac…
document subkey c6248ede37777df81f464960b119e247…
all distinct    true (want true)
stable          true (want true)

one master key in memory, one derived key per purpose, and no way to
get from a derived key back to the master or across to a sibling

---- rotation ----
old kid decrypts  true (want true)
new kid decrypts  true (want true)
ciphertexts differ true (want true)

same plaintext, same nonce, different key id — and the ciphertexts
share nothing, because the key id feeds the key derivation

---- nonce reuse, the one that is fatal ----
ct_a xor ct_b = 00000000000000000000001404050000
pt_a xor pt_b = 00000000000000000000001404050000
identical     true (want true)

the ciphertexts leaked the plaintext difference exactly. draw every
nonce from rand_bytes(12), or keep a counter you persist BEFORE use —
never after, or a crash replays one
